From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: plurx contributors <noreply@plurx.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] vo_gpu_next: map Dolby Vision with plurx's closed-form
 display management

A Profile 5 or 8 frame is reconstructed to BT.2020/PQ by libplacebo (the
RPU's reshaping and colour conversion only, as the plurx server renders
its master), then mapped for the display with plurx's closed-form CM v4.0
and CM v2.9 equations, creative trims included. The equations are
video/out/placebo/plurx/dv_dm.h and dm_closed_form.h, which the plurx
Player package copies from plurx's tools/dv_processing at build time, so
the player and the server run the same code. libdovi parses the RPU.

The target is the compositor's preferred description: its luminance range
and the largest named gamut (BT.2020, P3-D65, BT.709) inside the panel's
primaries, within half a step of the precision they were reported in. The
mapped frame and the swapchain hint carry that target's volume, so neither
libplacebo nor the compositor tone maps it again.

Each configuration logs one "plurx mapper:" line for the plurx Player
launcher. When the display is not in HDR mode or a frame cannot be mapped,
the line says why and libplacebo's own Dolby Vision handling renders it.
--dv-cm4-trims and --dv-cm29-trims (default yes) switch each family's
creative trims.
--dv-brightness=relative (default) maps on mpv's reference-white scale, so
the desktop's HDR brightness setting applies; absolute maps for the
panel's real luminance (the compositor's reference luminance, which
wayland_common now keeps, undoes mpv's rescaling) and scales the result
back.

A screenshot of a Dolby Vision frame is the same mapping for the last
presented target, so an unscaled screenshot with screenshot-tag-colorspace
is the post-mapping BT.2020/PQ frame.
